<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Venetian Ionian Islands</h1><p class="st-meta">1386 CE – 1797 CE · capital Corfu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Venice's possessions in the Ionian Sea, centred on Corfu, which stayed outside Ottoman rule and took on a lasting Italian character.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1386 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/corcyra">Corfu</a>, previously under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-house-of-anjou">Angevins of Naples</a>, placed itself under Venetian protection; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> formally bought the rights in 1402. It later added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakynthos">Zakynthos</a> (1485), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kefalonia">Cephalonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ithaca">Ithaca</a> (1500) and finally <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/leucas">Lefkada</a> (1684), while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kythira">Kythira</a> was already Venetian, gaining control over the sea route between the Adriatic and the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A Venetian provveditore generale in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/corcyra">Corfu</a> governed with local councils of island nobles registered in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libro-doro">Golden Book</a>. Italian became the language of administration and the elite, while the majority stayed Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">Olive oil</a> and currants were the main exports. Corfu's fortresses withstood Ottoman sieges in 1537 and 1716, the latter under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-matthias-von-der-schulenburg">Johann Matthias von der Schulenburg</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> ended the Venetian Republic in 1797, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-campo-formio">Treaty of Campo Formio</a> gave the islands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-ionian-islands">French Ionian Islands</a>. Their distinct Italian-Greek culture survived into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/septinsular-republic">Septinsular Republic</a> and British rule.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/venetian-ionian-islands.jpg" alt="Venetian Ionian Islands" width="320" height="301"><figcaption>Image: William Faden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Faden._Composite_Mediterranean._1785.R.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1386 CE – 1797 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Corfu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity, Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek, Venetian Italian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Venetian Corfu, Stato da Mar Ionian possessions</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1592&amp;focus=venetian-ionian-islands" aria-label="Venetian Ionian Islands on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5172 759 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Venetian Ionian Islands lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5492 1015L5293 831M5492 1015L5492 1015"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5293 831h0M5492 1015h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5492 1015h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5492 1015h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Corfu. <a href="/atlas?year=1592&amp;focus=venetian-ionian-islands">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
